course: dictionary
lesson: 13154
title: Forging: shaping, copying and building
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Forging has more than one meaning, so use the words around it to choose the right one. Ask yourself what would make sense in the sentence.
explain: «The blacksmith was forging a horseshoe in the hot fire.» Hot fire and horseshoe are the clues. Forging means heating metal and hammering it into shape.
explain: «The police said the signature on the cheque was forged.» Police and signature are the clues. Here forging means copying something to trick people, which is a crime.
explain: «The two girls were forging a friendship.» means building it slowly, with effort. «Mia kept forging ahead» means moving forward strongly. Swap your meaning into the sentence to check it still makes sense.
fact: Forging means shaping hot metal by heating it in a fire and hammering it.
fact: Forging can also mean making a copy of something, such as a signature or banknote, to trick people. This is dishonest and illegal.
fact: Forging a friendship means building it slowly, with effort.
fact: Forging ahead means moving forward strongly.
fact: A smith does the work of forging, and a forging is the metal piece that is made this way.
exercise: «The blacksmith was forging a horseshoe in the hot fire.» What is the blacksmith doing? | Painting a picture | Sleeping by the fire | Selling horseshoes in a shop | *Heating metal and hammering it into shape
exercise: «The police said the signature on the cheque was forged.» What does forged mean here? | Lost in the post | Written in neat handwriting | *Copied falsely to trick people | Painted in bright colours
exercise: «Mia kept forging ahead, passing one runner after another.» What does forging ahead mean? | *Moving forward strongly | Singing loudly | Stopping to rest | Going backwards
test: «The two girls spent all term forging a friendship by sharing and helping.» What does forging a friendship mean? | Finding a lost friend | Breaking up with a friend | Buying a new friend | *Building a friendship with effort
test: Mum says «Never forge my name on a school letter.» Why not? | Letters are made of metal | Forms are only for grown-ups, so it does not matter | *Copying someone's name to trick people is dishonest and illegal | Names are too long to write neatly
exam: Which use of forging is a crime? | Forging a friendship with a new classmate | Forging a horseshoe in a hot fire | *Forging a parent's signature on a letter | Forging ahead in a race
